\begin{abstract}
Understanding the dark sector remains one of the central challenges in modern
physics. We introduce the Autonomous Dark Domain Engine (ADDE-v4), a sovereign
computational infrastructure that integrates encrypted astrophysical data
ingestion, large-scale cosmological simulation, and real-time anomaly detection
within a unified framework. ADDE-v4 combines CKKS homomorphic encryption with
AMR+PM+Octree N-body solvers, enabling privacy-preserving analysis of weak-lensing
shear fields, BAO correlations, and generative anomaly metrics across 16{,}384
encrypted SIMD channels. Stress tests demonstrate stable evolution of
$16{,}384^3$ particles, near-peak network saturation, and automated cryptographic
bootstrapping at multiplicative depth~12. Under severe observational conditions
($30\%$ intrinsic shape noise, $25\%$ masking), ADDE-v4 reconstructs convergence
maps with $\mathrm{SNR}=8.42$ and identifies injected dark-sector perturbations
at $5.12\sigma$. These results establish ADDE-v4 as a next-generation scientific
instrument capable of securely probing dark matter and dark energy phenomena
across high-volume survey environments.
\end{abstract}